Fairphone is a Dutch social enterprise that designs modular smartphones specifically for longevity, repairability, and circularity. The Fairphone consists of 7 independently replaceable modules — display, battery, camera, speaker, USB-C port, earpiece, and back cover — each of which can be swapped by the user with a standard screwdriver, eliminating adhesives and proprietary tools. This modular architecture directly enables remanufacturing: returned devices can be quickly disassembled, individual modules tested and reconditioned or replaced, and the phone reassembled to like-new condition. Fairphone's take-back programme collects used devices for refurbishment or component harvesting — working modules become spare parts, extending the useful life of the entire product ecosystem. The company reports that extending a smartphone's lifespan by two years reduces its carbon footprint by 30%. The Fairphone 5 achieved "e-waste neutral" status, with an equal weight of e-waste responsibly recycled for every unit sold. Fairphone demonstrates how designing for modularity, standardisation, and interchangeability from the outset can make consumer electronics genuinely circular.